// Crusaders Mainframe — the operator settings that ride the heartbeat's
// `config` object, adopted once and clamped here.
//
// WHY THESE ARE IN A DEVICE-AGNOSTIC LIBRARY AT ALL
// -------------------------------------------------
// The name says LCD and half of them are not about one. What they have in
// common is not the hardware: it is that every one is a NUMBER AN ADMIN TYPES
// INTO A WEB FORM and a board then has to survive. A second Crusaders device
// with a screen, a scanner and a relay wants all nine unchanged, and wants the
// same three rules applied to them — which is `src/iot/README.md`'s test for
// this directory, so they live here rather than in one box's `lib/`.
//
// What stays out is the hardware itself: the LCD's COLUMN and ROW counts are a
// fact about the panel soldered to the board, not a setting, and they are not
// in this file for the same reason the relay cap is not (below).
//
// THE HOUSE RULE, NINE TIMES OVER
// -------------------------------
// The server may NARROW, the box CLAMPS, and an ABSENT key changes nothing.
//
//   * absent → that setting is left exactly where it was, so an older API
//     cannot blank what a newer one configured. Per KEY, not per response: a
//     `config` carrying one of the nine adopts one and leaves eight alone.
//   * out of range → CLAMPED, never refused. A club that typed 600 into one
//     field must not thereby lose the eight settings beside it, which is the
//     same sentence `RosterSync.h` writes about one bad booking and the
//     evening's other fifteen.
//   * unreadable — a quoted integer, a null, an array — is reported as ABSENCE
//     rather than as a default, because a default here BLANKS a setting an
//     admin chose.
//
// WHAT IS NEVER SERVER-CONFIGURABLE, AND WHY THERE IS NO KEY FOR IT
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// The relay HARD CAP, the relay MINIMUM, the AUTH MAXIMUM, the `!SHOW` hold
// clamp of 120 s and the LCD's column/row counts. `RelayGuard.h` argues it for
// the cap — the Mega's timer is only independent of the ESP if the Mega does
// not take the ESP's number on trust — and the argument transfers unchanged:
// a database row an admin can edit, reachable through an API, is not a safety
// bound. A bound that can be raised from a web form is not a bound. There is
// deliberately no key for any of them, and adding one is not a settings change
// but a removal of the last thing standing between a misconfigured club and a
// court that burns all night.
//
// THE CROSS-KEY INVARIANT
// -----------------------
// `scan_memory_seconds >= lcd_choice_seconds`, enforced by RAISING the memory
// and never by lowering the choice window. See kScanMemoryMaxSeconds below for
// what it costs when it does not hold.
//
// No Arduino headers, no heap, no String. Compiles for AVR, Xtensa and the
// host, and at gnu++11 for the generated sketches — so no multi-statement
// constexpr function lives here.

#ifndef CRUSADERS_DEVLINK_LCDSETTINGS_H
#define CRUSADERS_DEVLINK_LCDSETTINGS_H

#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>

// --- the compile-time fallbacks ---------------------------------------------
//
// Build flags as well as settings, for the reason every other timing in this
// tree is one: `tools/strict_compile.sh` compiles these headers with no
// PlatformIO flags at all and so does any editor, and a header that only
// compiles under one build system is a header nobody can read in isolation.
// A board that has never heard from the API runs on these.

/// How long the LCD holds a transient message before reverting to idle.
#ifndef LCD_REVERT_MS
#define LCD_REVERT_MS 10000UL
#endif

/// How long the walk-up pad keeps a member's selection on screen.
#ifndef LCD_CHOICE_MS
#define LCD_CHOICE_MS 20000UL
#endif

/// How long a scanned card's acknowledgement stays up.
#ifndef LCD_CARD_MS
#define LCD_CARD_MS 2000UL
#endif

/// How long a one-line hint stays up.
#ifndef LCD_HINT_MS
#define LCD_HINT_MS 3000UL
#endif

/// How long the bridge remembers WHO scanned. Already a court-box build flag
/// (`platformio.ini`) and cross-asserted by `courtlogic/AuthWindow.h` and
/// `protocol/Protocol.h`; the value here is the same 30 s, so a translation
/// unit that sees both this header and those keeps agreeing with itself.
#ifndef AUTH_TTL_MS
#define AUTH_TTL_MS 30000UL
#endif

/// How long a board keeps believing a clock it has not re-synchronised.
#ifndef CLOCK_TRUST_MS
#define CLOCK_TRUST_MS 5400000UL
#endif

/// Whether this board re-energises relays from a served restore at all.
#ifndef RELAY_RESTORE
#define RELAY_RESTORE 1
#endif

/// Whether this board reports faults at all.
///
/// DUPLICATED FROM `gatelogic/FaultLog.h`, WHICH SAYS SO ITSELF: that header
/// records that the fault log belongs in `shared/lib` by this tree's own test
/// and that merging the two is owed. The values are kept identical (1 and 20)
/// so a translation unit including both sees one answer, whichever `#ifndef`
/// wins. The merge is the fix; a second value here would be the defect.
#ifndef FAULT_REPORT
#define FAULT_REPORT 1
#endif

/// How many fault EVENTS this board may queue per code per hour.
#ifndef FAULT_MAX_PER_HOUR
#define FAULT_MAX_PER_HOUR 20
#endif

namespace devlink {

// --- the bounds, in the units the wire carries -------------------------------
//
// Clamped in SECONDS (or MINUTES) BEFORE the multiply, exactly as
// `parseHeartbeatInterval` is: bounding after the multiply lets a body
// claiming 4,300,000 wrap a uint32_t into a plausible small number, and a
// plausible small number is acted on rather than questioned.

inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdRevertMinSeconds = 3U;
inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdRevertMaxSeconds = 60U;

/// Five seconds is the floor because the pad is a HUMAN interface: below it a
/// member cannot read their own selection, and the invariant below would drag
/// the scan memory down with it.
inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdChoiceMinSeconds = 5U;
inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdChoiceMaxSeconds = 60U;

inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdCardMinSeconds = 1U;
inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdCardMaxSeconds = 10U;

inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdHintMinSeconds = 1U;
inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdHintMaxSeconds = 10U;

/// Five minutes of remembered identity is already generous; the ceiling exists
/// because the memory is an AUTHORISATION and an unexpiring one is a court
/// anybody can buy on somebody else's token by waiting for them to leave.
///
/// THE FLOOR IS NOT A CONSTANT — it is `lcd_choice_seconds`, whatever that
/// currently is. If the pad outlives the memory, a member who scans, wanders
/// off and comes back finds their selection still showing while the bridge has
/// forgotten who they are, and the next person's presses buy on that member's
/// token. `kLcdChoiceMinSeconds` is the smallest the floor can ever be.
inline constexpr uint32_t kScanMemoryMaxSeconds = 300U;

inline constexpr uint32_t kClockTrustMinMinutes = 5U;
inline constexpr uint32_t kClockTrustMaxMinutes = 1440U;

inline constexpr uint16_t kMinFaultMaxPerHour = 1U;
inline constexpr uint16_t kMaxFaultMaxPerHour = 120U;

// --- the compile-time fallbacks as values ------------------------------------
//
// As values rather than macros, so the struct the orchestration is written
// against is the one the board actually builds.

inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdRevertDefaultMs = LCD_REVERT_MS;
inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdChoiceDefaultMs = LCD_CHOICE_MS;
inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdCardDefaultMs = LCD_CARD_MS;
inline constexpr uint32_t kLcdHintDefaultMs = LCD_HINT_MS;
inline constexpr uint32_t kScanMemoryDefaultMs = AUTH_TTL_MS;
inline constexpr uint32_t kClockTrustDefaultMs = CLOCK_TRUST_MS;
inline constexpr bool kRelayRestoreDefault = (RELAY_RESTORE) != 0;
inline constexpr bool kFaultReportingDefault = (FAULT_REPORT) != 0;
inline constexpr uint16_t kFaultMaxPerHourDefault = static_cast<uint16_t>(FAULT_MAX_PER_HOUR);

// Every compile-time fallback must be inside the range a heartbeat may set, or
// the board starts at a value it can never be told to return to — which is the
// assertion `gatelogic/FaultLog.h` already makes about its own two.
static_assert(kLcdRevertDefaultMs >= kLcdRevertMinSeconds * 1000UL &&
                  kLcdRevertDefaultMs <= kLcdRevertMaxSeconds * 1000UL,
              "LCD_REVERT_MS is outside the range a heartbeat may set");
static_assert(kLcdChoiceDefaultMs >= kLcdChoiceMinSeconds * 1000UL &&
                  kLcdChoiceDefaultMs <= kLcdChoiceMaxSeconds * 1000UL,
              "LCD_CHOICE_MS is outside the range a heartbeat may set");
static_assert(kLcdCardDefaultMs >= kLcdCardMinSeconds * 1000UL &&
                  kLcdCardDefaultMs <= kLcdCardMaxSeconds * 1000UL,
              "LCD_CARD_MS is outside the range a heartbeat may set");
static_assert(kLcdHintDefaultMs >= kLcdHintMinSeconds * 1000UL &&
                  kLcdHintDefaultMs <= kLcdHintMaxSeconds * 1000UL,
              "LCD_HINT_MS is outside the range a heartbeat may set");
static_assert(kScanMemoryDefaultMs <= kScanMemoryMaxSeconds * 1000UL,
              "AUTH_TTL_MS is longer than the scan memory a heartbeat may set");
static_assert(kClockTrustDefaultMs >= kClockTrustMinMinutes * 60000UL &&
                  kClockTrustDefaultMs <= kClockTrustMaxMinutes * 60000UL,
              "CLOCK_TRUST_MS is outside the range a heartbeat may set");
static_assert(kFaultMaxPerHourDefault >= kMinFaultMaxPerHour &&
                  kFaultMaxPerHourDefault <= kMaxFaultMaxPerHour,
              "FAULT_MAX_PER_HOUR is outside the range a heartbeat may set");

/// THE CROSS-KEY INVARIANT, at compile time as well as at runtime. Today it
/// holds by accident — 30 against 20 — and an accident is not an invariant:
/// the next person to shorten one flag would find out at a court, from a
/// member who was charged for somebody else's booking.
static_assert(kScanMemoryDefaultMs >= kLcdChoiceDefaultMs,
              "AUTH_TTL_MS is shorter than LCD_CHOICE_MS, so this board would forget who scanned "
              "while their selection is still on the pad and the next person's presses would buy "
              "on their token");

/// The nine settings, in the units the board uses rather than the units the
/// wire carries. Default-constructed to the compile-time fallbacks, which is
/// what a board that has never heard from the API runs on.
///
/// Held across heartbeats by the caller — that is what makes "an absent key
/// changes nothing" mean anything at all.
struct LcdSettings {
  /// `lcd_revert_seconds`.
  uint32_t revertMs = kLcdRevertDefaultMs;
  /// `lcd_choice_seconds`.
  uint32_t choiceMs = kLcdChoiceDefaultMs;
  /// `lcd_card_seconds`.
  uint32_t cardMs = kLcdCardDefaultMs;
  /// `lcd_hint_seconds`.
  uint32_t hintMs = kLcdHintDefaultMs;
  /// `scan_memory_seconds`, never below `choiceMs`.
  uint32_t scanMemoryMs = kScanMemoryDefaultMs;
  /// `clock_trust_minutes`.
  uint32_t clockTrustMs = kClockTrustDefaultMs;
  /// `relay_restore_enabled`.
  bool relayRestoreEnabled = kRelayRestoreDefault;
  /// `device_fault_reporting`.
  bool faultReporting = kFaultReportingDefault;
  /// `device_fault_max_per_hour`.
  uint16_t faultMaxPerHour = kFaultMaxPerHourDefault;
};

/// Adopts whichever of the nine the heartbeat response's TOP-LEVEL `config`
/// object carries, each clamped, into `out`.
///
/// True when at least one of the nine was read. False — with every setting
/// left where it was — when there is no top-level `config` object, when it is
/// not an object, when the body is cut off before it balances, or when it
/// carries none of the nine.
///
/// <b>Read one level deep, from the top-level `config` and nowhere else.</b>
/// `devapi::json::findKey` is a flat scan by design and is depended upon
/// elsewhere, but it takes the FIRST match in document order — so a flat read
/// would adopt a number out of a vendor block, a diagnostics object or
/// anything a later contract nests, whichever came first in the bytes. That is
/// round G4's B2 with a screen timeout instead of a lock, and `adoptUnlockMs`
/// wrote the answer down before this existed: find the object, then read its
/// OWN members. Bounded at both ends, so a key added to the document after the
/// object closed is not adopted either.
///
/// <b>The cross-key invariant is re-established on every call</b>, including
/// the false one, because it has to hold across heartbeats and not merely
/// within one: a later response that raises only `lcd_choice_seconds` has to
/// bring the scan memory it was already carrying up with it. A struct that
/// already holds the invariant is untouched by that step.
bool parseLcdSettings(const char* body, size_t len, LcdSettings& out);

} // namespace devlink

#endif // CRUSADERS_DEVLINK_LCDSETTINGS_H
